## 2. Sliding-window overlap produces short, largely duplicated final chunks (`esg_chunker.py`, corpus-wide)

**Status:** Open — documented for engineering follow-up (not fixed)
**Found by:** Automated QA (`qa3`), Tier 3 (Chunking Quality) checks
**Date logged:** 2026-07-30
**Severity:** Medium — inflates the index with near-duplicate text and can starve retrieval of new content at section tails; does not corrupt citations or violate the documented token bounds
**Related automated checks:** Tier 3, Check 15 (Terminal-chunk effect: token count by position)
**Component:** `esg_chunker.py` — specifically `chunk_token_ranges()` (esg/src/esg_chunker.py:511-536), constants `CHUNK_SIZE = 500`, `OVERLAP = 50`, `MIN_CHUNK_TOKENS = 100`, `MAX_CHUNK_TOKENS = 600` (esg/src/esg_chunker.py:23-26)
**Example document:** `CRMT-AMERICA'S CAR-MART INC-2023` (America's Car-Mart, 2023 Responsibility Report), section `community__0001`

**Evidence location:**
Chunk files: `data/04_chunks/esg/CRMT/CRMT-AMERICA'S CAR-MART INC-2023__community__0001__chunk_0004.txt` and `..._chunk_0005.txt`
Index row: `data/00_reference/esg_chunks_index.csv`, `chunk_id = CRMT__CRMT_AMERICA_S_CAR_MART_INC_2023__community__0001__chunk_0005`

### How this was found

Tier 3 QA Check 15 ("terminal-chunk effect") compares mean token count by a chunk's position within its section (first / middle / last / only). Across the whole corpus (32,581 sections with 2+ chunks):

| position | n | min | p05 | median | mean | p95 | max |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| first | 10,777 | 498 | 499 | 500 | 499.89 | 500 | 500 |
| middle | 13,720 | 497 | 499 | 500 | 499.92 | 501 | 503 |
| last | 10,777 | 149 | 166 | 330 | 345.51 | 567 | 600 |

Welch's t-test on last vs. middle: t = −124.41, p ≈ 0 (mean 345.5 vs. 499.9 tokens). The effect size and sample size (n=10,777 on both sides) rule out sampling noise — this is a systematic property of every multi-chunk section, not an occasional glitch.

One of the flagged examples (`token_count = 149`, the minimum observed for a "normal" last chunk) was pulled for manual inspection: CRMT's `community__0001` section, chunk index 5.

### Summary of the defect

`chunk_token_ranges()` builds chunks with a fixed-size sliding window: each chunk spans 500 tokens, and the window advances by only `500 − OVERLAP(50) = 450` tokens per step, so consecutive chunks intentionally share ~50 tokens of context. This produces exact, tightly-clustered 500-token chunks for every `first` and `middle` position — confirmed by the table above (min 497, max 503 across both).

The **last** chunk of a section is different: it is whatever is left over once the window can no longer advance a full step before hitting the end of the section. That leftover has no fixed size — it is a function of `section_length mod 450` (roughly), which is why its size is smeared uniformly from 100 up to 600 tokens rather than clustering anywhere.

Manual inspection of the CRMT example shows the practical consequence: the 149-token final chunk is **not** 149 tokens of new content. Its opening ~100 tokens are a verbatim repeat of chunk 4's tail (the 50-token overlap plus the fact that the "new" step distance was small relative to the window), and only its last few sentences are text that chunk 4 didn't already contain:

```
chunk_0004 (tail):
  ...Economics Arkansas / SOAR Northwest Arkansas / Springdale Public
  School Foundation / Various community food banks / Athletic programs /
  Financial literacy efforts / Teacher appreciation events / Corporate
  Citizenship / Members of our management team, including local deal[ers...]

chunk_0005 (full, 149 tokens):
  Economics Arkansas / SOAR Northwest Arkansas / Springdale Public
  School Foundation / Various community food banks / Athletic programs /
  Financial literacy efforts / Teacher appreciation events / Corporate
  Citizenship / Members of our management team, including local
  dealerships, also serve as volunteers or board members for various
  nonprofit groups. Our Chief Executive Officer Emeritus, Jeff Williams,
  currently serves on the boards of directors for Mercy Health Northwest
  Arkansas Communities since 2019 and the Jones Center and Jones Trust
  since 2021. Jeff is passionate about the Company's vision of improving
  the communities we serve.

  America's Car-Mart Responsibility Report
  24
  Environmental: Respecting our Footprint
```

Everything before "dealerships, also serve as volunteers..." is a duplicate of chunk 4. In this example, roughly two-thirds of a 149-token chunk is recycled overlap text, not new evidence.

### Why this is a problem for retrieval

- **Index bloat / near-duplication:** a meaningful fraction of `last`-position chunks are mostly restatements of the immediately preceding chunk, inflating the index without adding retrievable information.
- **Thin evidence at section tails:** when a `last` chunk *is* retrieved on its own, the model may see mostly text it would also see from the preceding chunk, with only a small amount of genuinely new tail content — the opposite of what chunking is meant to provide.
- **Not fixable by simple merging:** a `last` chunk near the low end (e.g., 149 tokens) plus its preceding `middle` chunk (~500 tokens) would total ~650 tokens, which exceeds the chunker's own `MAX_CHUNK_TOKENS = 600` ceiling (esg/src/esg_chunker.py:26). So "just glue the tail onto the previous chunk" is not a valid fix under the current bounds — this would need a rebalancing strategy (e.g., splitting the final ~600–1000 tokens of a section into two more evenly sized chunks instead of greedy 500-token windows plus a leftover), not a one-line patch.

### Scope / how widespread this might be

The distributional evidence (t-test, full quantile table) is **corpus-wide**, not a single-document estimate — it covers all 10,777 sections in the corpus that produced a `last` chunk. The manual walkthrough above verifies the mechanism against exactly one example (CRMT `community__0001`, chunk 5); the other four examples surfaced by the same check (DECK `environmental__0008` chunk 3, T `community__0001` chunk 2, BBBY `waste__0002` chunk 2, CVS `environmental__0001` chunk 1 — all at or near 149-150 tokens) have not yet been individually opened to confirm they show the same overlap-heavy pattern, and should not be assumed identical without checking. Note also `short_evidence_share_at_last_position = 0.0` in the same check output — none of these short `last` chunks are being caught by the existing `short_evidence` classification, because 149+ tokens is still above the `MIN_CHUNK_TOKENS = 100` floor used to define that category. This defect is invisible to every check that only looks at whether token counts fall inside [100, 600].
